Item 4 also: "逐对象祖先遍历权限、reparse/hardlink/ADS 与 ACL 继承是否有 fail-closed 闭包" — r2 line 24 covers: ancestors registered, traverse/read-execute only, "路径不存在、reparse/hardlink/alternate stream/ACL 继承与预期不符，或无法证明权限交集时停止" ✓. Also line 19 covers the profile root ancestors. Also line 28 requires re-verifying recursive reparse state. So the closure is stated. Remaining gap: **"ACL 继承与预期不符" is not defined as a test** — what is "预期"? There's no requirement to record the baseline inherited ACE set/baseline DACL digest per object, nor to *disable inheritance* on the grant objects (adding a Package SID ACE to a directory with inheritable ACEs propagates to the whole subtree — r2 line 24 says "每一条被授权路径的完整祖先也必须逐项登记；祖先最多授予必要的 traverse/read-execute，不授 create/write/delete" but doesn't say the grant ACEs must be **non-inheritable** (no OI/CI) so they can't silently propagate to unregistered descendants). That's a strong, concrete ACL-precision finding: a Package SID read ACE placed on `C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\2022\BuildTools` with inheritance would propagate to all descendants (fine for RO roots, but the same technique on, e.g., the scratch clone root with "read" would... hmm). More important: for the *write* grants on scratch subdirs, using inheritable ACEs is needed. The real risk: a grant on an ancestor (e.g., traverse/read-execute on `G:\CODEX`) could carry inheritance and effectively grant broad access. r2 says ancestors get at most traverse/read-execute, but does not require those ACEs to be non-inheritable, nor that inheritance be re-evaluated on each descendant. Also, "只授 traverse" is not separable in Windows: there's no "traverse-only" ACE for directories; traverse is allowed via `SeChangeNotifyPrivilege` (bypass traverse checking), which every interactive user token holds by default — so a "traverse-only" ACE doesn't exist; you grant FILE_TRAVERSE (0x20) as part of the directory ACE, or rely on bypass-traverse. And critically, the AppContainer/LPAC token — does it have SeChangeNotifyPrivilege? AppContainer tokens do hold SeChangeNotifyPrivilege I believe. Hmm: If LPAC lacks SeChangeNotifyPrivilege, it needs FILE_TRAVERSE on every ancestor; if it has it, no ancestor ACE is needed except where the object itself must be opened. r2's requirement to "登记祖先 traverse" is thus ambiguous given bypass-traverse semantics. This is a legit precision finding (P2): the "祖先只授 traverse/read-execute" requirement is not directly expressible; Windows has no traverse-only ACE and interactive tokens normally hold SeChangeNotifyPrivilege (bypass traverse), so the contract must instead require (a) recording whether the LPAC token has SeChangeNotifyPrivilege, and (b) non-inheritable explicit ACEs on the exact object, and (c) an AccessCheck that includes the ancestor path components. Good.

