Now also: r2 line 101 PATH: "仅本清单的 Node、Python/venv、Rust、Git、VS/SDK 只读根与经审查的 Windows loader 所需目录" — but the system loader dirs are exactly the unresolved part (item 7). Also: the LPAC process needs to resolve the executable via PATH; with no `C:\Windows\System32` in PATH, `cmd.exe` etc. can't be found. And `PATH` including System32 means an LPAC would attempt to read System32 — which is denied without ACE... meaning PATH entries that are inaccessible are just failures. This ties to item 7: the contract forbids broad Windows authorization yet the LPAC's runtime needs (loader + any cmd.exe usage, plus the VC++ runtime redistributables in System32, plus `python312.dll` load chain, plus `.NET`? no) are effectively "System32 + WinSxS read". Practically, this means the LPAC design likely cannot run real builds without either (a) granting ALL RESTRICTED APPLICATION PACKAGES / Package SID read on large portions of `C:\Windows` (a broad authorization r2 explicitly forbids), or (b) an infeasible per-file enumeration. So the honest conclusion for item 7: this is NOT an implementable precise scope as written; it is a bounded-fallback-free requirement over an unbounded dynamic set ⇒ the contract must either (i) define a *measured* closure: run the synthetic probe with a documented, explicitly enumerated initial system-read set derived from the *actual* loader trace, then freeze the observed set as the definitive registry and re-run — i.e., a two-phase "trace-then-freeze" procedure with the trace itself as the authorization basis, plus (ii) state that the ACEs on system files are (a) read-only, (b) non-inheritable, (c) captured/restored, (d) require elevation or are impossible ⇒ if impossible, the LPAC direction must be re-scoped (e.g., accept regular AppContainer or move to a VM). Also note LPAC's documented need for `registryRead` capability to read any registry key: with zero capabilities the LPAC may fail even basic startup reads of HKCU/HKLM needed by Python/Torch (e.g. read